<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Analyzers must target netstandard2.0: that is what the compiler loads. -->
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- Packable as an ordinary library: Ashlar.Infrastructure consumes this assembly at
         RUNTIME (AnalyzerFenceGate instantiates the catalog), so the packed hosting graph
         needs it in the local feed. In-repo projects additionally attach it in the
         analyzer role via ProjectReference; package consumers only need the library. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>true</EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>
    <PackageId>Ashlar.Analyzers</PackageId>
    <Title>Ashlar Trust-Loop Analyzers</Title>
    <Description>Roslyn rules for Ashlar brick code: contract drift, constructor purity, determinism, and the certification analyzer-gate catalog.</Description>
    <PackageTags>ashlar;analyzers;roslyn;trust-loop;certification</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- netstandard2.0 leg of the contracts package. Safe for the build-time analyzer role:
         the only type using it (BrickConstraintManifestAnalyzer) carries no [DiagnosticAnalyzer]
         attribute, so compiler discovery never instantiates it — it is constructed
         programmatically by the certification gate WITH a manifest instance (spec A2.3:
         the manifest object itself is the single source, used twice).
         Consumers reference THIS project with UndefineProperties="TargetFramework" so a
         host's global -p:TargetFramework (e.g. the CLI image) never reaches this
         netstandard2.0-only subtree; nearest-TF negotiation then picks the contracts
         package's ns2.0 leg here normally. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ashlar.Brick.Contracts\Ashlar.Brick.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="AnalyzerReleases.Shipped.md" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="AnalyzerReleases.Unshipped.md" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE ANALYZER ROLE, IN THE PACKAGE.

    This package shipped its assembly ONLY under lib/netstandard2.0/. NuGet loads analyzers from
    analyzers/dotnet/cs/ and nowhere else, so a `<PackageReference Include="Ashlar.Analyzers" />`
    added every rule to the compile-time reference set and ran none of them. The analyzer fence —
    one of the certification gate's five legs — was therefore silently inert for every brick author
    consuming Ashlar from nuget.org, which is the whole external audience. Nothing said so: the
    build was clean, because a rule that never runs never fires.

    The assembly is packed into BOTH locations, deliberately:
      * lib/netstandard2.0/ — Ashlar.Infrastructure references this assembly as an ordinary
        library at RUNTIME (AnalyzerFenceGate constructs the catalog and attaches it to candidate
        compilations). Removing the lib/ copy would break the packed hosting graph.
      * analyzers/dotnet/cs/ — the only path the C# compiler looks at. This is the copy that makes
        the fence actually run in a consumer's build.
    They are the same file in two roles, which is exactly what this project already is in-repo:
    every in-tree consumer references it with OutputItemType="Analyzer" AND
    ReferenceOutputAssembly="true".

    CONSEQUENCE FOR BRICK AUTHORS, stated plainly because it was once stated wrongly. Because the
    lib/ leg is real, a PackageReference to this package DOES put an assembly into the referencing
    project's compile set and output. It is NOT true that "the analyzers put nothing into the built
    brick" — that is only true of the shape BrickDependencyChecker requires:

        <PackageReference Include="Ashlar.Analyzers" Version="..." ExcludeAssets="runtime;compile" />

    PrivateAssets="all" does NOT achieve it: PrivateAssets governs transitive flow to YOUR
    consumers, not what YOUR project receives. ExcludeAssets="runtime;compile" leaves the
    `analyzers` asset group untouched, so the rules still run, while the DLL stays out of the brick.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(OutputPath)$(AssemblyName).dll"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs"
          Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
